WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.152
Operators monitor the indicators their approved system exposes.

00:00:04.152 --> 00:00:12.674
These may include receiver warnings, satellite status, accuracy estimates, signal-quality summaries, flight-mode changes, home-point messages, and telemetry trends.

00:00:12.674 --> 00:00:16.389
Satellite count alone is not a verdict.

00:00:16.389 --> 00:00:20.104
Geometry, quality, obstruction, and receiver design matter.

00:00:20.104 --> 00:00:29.500
A high count can coexist with a poor solution, while a temporary low count may have a benign environmental explanation.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.467
Compare independent cues.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:40.426
Does the map agree with visible landmarks, aircraft movement, compass or heading, known route, camera view, and crew observations?

00:00:40.426 --> 00:00:45.889
Disagreement raises concern even when no single cue identifies the cause.

00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:49.604
Trends are more useful than isolated values.

00:00:49.604 --> 00:01:02.059
Record when the anomaly began, where the aircraft was, which mode was active, what changed, and whether behavior recovered after landing or moving away from the area.

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:09.707
Preserve logs, warnings, screenshots, timestamps, weather, site details, aircraft configuration, and crew notes according to policy.

00:01:09.707 --> 00:01:16.481
Do not manipulate the system or repeat the flight merely to reproduce suspected interference.

00:01:16.481 --> 00:01:18.011
Classify carefully.

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:23.474
A suspicious indicator is not a confirmed jamming or spoofing incident.

00:01:23.474 --> 00:01:32.433
Reports should state observed symptoms, confidence, benign alternatives, safety actions, and evidence available for qualified technical or regulatory review.
